{"name":"Royal Purple","hex":"#4b006e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"royal purple","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deep vibrant reddish purple colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a deep vibrant reddish purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/royal_purple"}],"entries":[{"title":"Royal Purple (lubricant manufacturer)","blocks":[{"text":"Royal Purple is an American manufacturer which produces lubricants for automotive, industrial, marine, and racing use. It is known primarily for its line of synthetic Royal Purple Motor Oil products used in gasoline and diesel engines. They also produce other fluids including gear oil, transmission fluid, power steering fluid and industrial gear, bearing, engine, and hydraulic oil. Other products include chassis and bearing grease, lubricant aerosol, aerosol chain lubricant, and firearm lubricant."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7d/Royal_purple_logo_fair_use.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Purple_(lubricant_manufacturer)","wikidata":"Q7374748"},{"title":"Royal Purple (newspaper)","blocks":[{"text":"Royal Purple is a student-run weekly newspaper at the University of Wisconsin–Whitewater in Whitewater, Wisconsin. It publishes online every Monday throughout the academic year, as well as quarterly seasonal print issues at 75 locations across the campus, city and at the UW-Whitewater at Rock County extension."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4e/Royal_Purple_Newspaper_Logo.jpg/330px-Royal_Purple_Newspaper_Logo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Purple_(newspaper)","wikidata":"Q7374749"},{"title":"Tyrian purple","blocks":[{"text":"Tyrian purple, also known as royal purple, imperial purple, imperial dye, or simply tyrian, is a reddish-purple natural dye. It is secreted by several species of predatory sea snails in the family Muricidae, rock snails originally known by the name murex. The coloured compound is 6,6'-dibromoindigo."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Purple_Purpur_%28retouched%29.jpg/330px-Purple_Purpur_%28retouched%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrian_purple","wikidata":"Q5180360"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple","wikidata":"Q3257809"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/PM_110511_Liebig_Chromos.jpg/330px-PM_110511_Liebig_Chromos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/PM_110511_Liebig_Chromos.jpg/330px-PM_110511_Liebig_Chromos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 20th-century depiction of a Roman triumph celebrated by Julius Caesar. Caesar, riding in the chariot, wears the solid Tyrian purple toga picta. In the foreground, two Roman magistrates are identified by their toga praetexta, white with a stripe of Tyrian purple.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APM_110511_Liebig_Chromos.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Haustellum_brandaris_000.jpg/500px-Haustellum_brandaris_000.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Haustellum_brandaris_000.jpg/1280px-Haustellum_brandaris_000.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Haustellum_brandaris_000.jpg/500px-Haustellum_brandaris_000.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two shells of Bolinus brandaris, the spiny dye-murex, a source of the dye","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHaustellum_brandaris_000.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Potsherds_in_Aperlae.jpg/500px-Potsherds_in_Aperlae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Potsherds_in_Aperlae.jpg/1280px-Potsherds_in_Aperlae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Potsherds_in_Aperlae.jpg/500px-Potsherds_in_Aperlae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Murex shells at the site of purple dye production in ancient Aperlae, Lycia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APotsherds_in_Aperlae.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Mosaic_of_Justinianus_I_-_Basilica_San_Vitale_%28Ravenna%29.jpg/500px-Mosaic_of_Justinianus_I_-_Basilica_San_Vitale_%28Ravenna%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Mosai